"""Basit's own narration for MR MONEY (second recording): one entry per scene.

text   = what he says in the chosen take (also the on-screen captions), with names spelt correctly.
rec    = which phone recording holds the take: "n<k>" = second recording session (Rec1, Rec_3, Rec_4, New_rec_5..7, Rec_8),
         "o<k>" = first session (rec2..rec8), used where the second session slipped or left a line out
         (the last good take in the file is chosen automatically).
span   = exact word range (i, j) of the recording's transcript, when the matcher can't pick the right take.
start  = begin the cut at this word (drops a slip before it).   end = stop the cut after this word (drops a slip after it).
Fact fixes made in the edit: y6 drops the day (every take says "18th"; the album came out on 8 September).
"""
L = {
    "o1": ("n4", "September 2022. Lagos. A boy is lying on the floor of his father's parlour, staring at the ceiling."),
    "o2": ("n3", "WAEC done. JAMB done. And nobody can tell him what comes next. Not even him."),
    "o3": ("n1", "Then a song comes on. And for the first time in months, something in his chest moves. Hope.", dict(span=(13, 29))),
    "o4": ("n1", "The album is called Mr Money with the Vibe. And the man who made it? His own father once told him no."),
    "o5": ("n1", "His father said no. Olamide didn't reply for almost two years. A crowd crush nearly ended everything. And he still became one of the biggest voices in Afrobeats. This is how."),
    "a1": ("n5", "His name is Ahmed Ololade. Born on the thirteenth of January, 1995, in Lagos."),
    "a2": ("n1", "And here's the crazy part. In Yoruba, Ololade means wealth has arrived. Long before anyone called him Mr Money, his own name was already saying it."),
    "a3": ("n5", "The world calls him Asake. To most Nigerians, that's a woman's name. Many reports say it comes from his mother."),
    "a4": ("o4", "He grew up a performer, a dancer. But the people around him noticed something else about him. Something that would one day cost him his home."),
    "g1": ("n5", "They called him Giran. In Yoruba, it means stubborn."),
    "g2": ("n5", "At fifteen, he wanted music. His father said no. So the boy left home."),
    "g3": ("o5", "Years later, he would laugh about it. The father who gave birth to me, he said, is more stubborn than I am."),
    "g4": ("o4", "His mother tried another way. She gathered money. She got him an international passport. A way out, to study in the United Kingdom."),
    "g5": ("o5", "Most people would take it. He said no. He wanted Nigeria. It took the Yoruba actor Lala to convince his mother to let him study at Obafemi Awolowo University instead. Remember that. It changes everything."),
    "i1": ("n6", "At OAU, he studied Theatre and Performing Arts. He learned the stage long before the stage learned his name."),
    "i2": ("n6", "In 2018, he signed to a small label called TFT Records. He put out songs like Kanipe. And almost nobody noticed."),
    "i3": ("n5", "Then, in 2020, he released a song with a title sounding like a joke. Or a prophecy. Mr Money."),
    "i4": ("n6", "That same year, he started texting one man. The one man he believed could change his life. Olamide. The boss of YBNL."),
    "i5": ("n5", "By Asake's own account, he texted. He begged for a long time. Nothing came back. Until one song did what all those texts could not."),
    "y1": ("n5", "That song was Omo Ope. Olamide heard it before it came out, and jumped on it with a verse of his own."),
    "y2": ("n5", "And then, as Asake tells it, the reply finally landed. Asake, we have to see."),
    "y3": ("n6", "February 2022. Olamide told the world. Welcome to YBNL."),
    "y4": ("n6", "What happened next wasn't a rise. It was a takeover."),
    "y5": ("n6", "Omo Ope. Sungba. A Sungba remix with Burna Boy. Peace Be Unto You. Terminator. Within months, his voice was everywhere, from the danfos of Lagos to the clubs of London."),
    "y6": ("n5", "September 2022. The debut album landed. Mr Money with the Vibe.", dict(start="september")),
    "y7": ("n6", "At the time, it broke the record for the biggest opening day by an African album on Apple Music. And it entered the Billboard 200 at number 66."),
    "y8": ("n6", "By December, Audiomack named him artiste of the year. 330 million streams."),
    "y9": ("n6", "And somewhere in Lagos, on a parlour floor, a boy with no plan pressed play. Remember him. He comes back."),
    "b1": ("n6", "December 2022. London. And the biggest week of his life was about to become the darkest."),
    "b2": ("n6", "Asake sold out three nights at the O2 Academy Brixton. On the final night, a crowd crush at the venue left several people critically injured."),
    "b3": ("n6", "Two of them never came home. Rebecca Ikumelo, 33, who came to see Asake perform. And Gaby Hutchinson, 23, a security guard working that night."),
    "w1": ("n6", "He kept moving. 2023: Yoga. 2:30. And Amapiano, with Olamide, which earned him his first Grammy nomination."),
    "w2": ("n6", "His second album, Work of Art, landed on the sixteenth of June, 2023. Number 66 on the Billboard 200, again. Number 20 in the UK."),
    "w3": ("n6", "That August, he went back to London and sold out the O2 Arena. He opened the night honouring the two who died at Brixton. Then he arrived by helicopter."),
    "w4": ("n6", "A month later, in New York, he became the first African artist to headline and sell out the Barclays Center."),
    "w5": ("n6", "The boy who refused to leave Nigeria was now filling the biggest rooms in the world. But the hardest test for a stubborn man was still coming. Leaving the house that made him."),
    "l1": ("n6", "2024. Album number three, Lungu Boy. Travis Scott. Central Cee. Stormzy. Wizkid. All on board."),
    "l2": ("n6", "Day one: 9.2 million streams. The biggest opening day for a Nigerian album on Spotify."),
    "l3": ("n7", "His song with Wizkid, MMS, set a new first-day record for a song on Spotify Nigeria. And it brought him a second Grammy nomination."),
    "v1": ("n7", "And then Giran did what Giran always does. He chose his own road."),
    "v2": ("n7", "The twelfth of February, 2025. A single called Why Love, on his own new label. Giran Republic. The stubborn republic."),
    "v3": ("n7", "It meant leaving YBNL, the house that first opened the door for him. But the bond held. Olamide later called him his brother for life."),
    "v4": ("n7", "And the father who once said no? After years of distance, Asake says the love is still the same."),
    "m1": ("n7", "Now watch the stubborn bet pay off. January 2026: Real, Volume One, with Wizkid. Straight to number one in Nigeria."),
    "m2": ("n7", "Then, on the first of May, 2026, album number four. M$NEY."),
    "m3": ("o8", "First week: 42.2 million streams on Spotify Nigeria. A record."),
    "m4": ("n8", "And that July, he stood on the stage of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane, in London, with a live orchestra and a forty-member choir. The first Afrobeats artist ever to headline it."),
    "t1": ("n8", "But numbers don't explain why a boy on the floor felt saved. So here's the real question. Why him? Why does Asake hit different?", dict(span=(33, 57))),
    "t2": ("n8", "Listen to what he mixes. Fuji, the sound that grew out of the songs that woke Lagos for Ramadan. Amapiano log drums from South Africa. Street pop. And behind it all, a choir.", dict(span=(57, 92))),
    "t3": ("n8", "He sings mostly in Yoruba, with street slang and pidgin. He doesn't translate himself for anybody. And the world comes to him anyway."),
    "t4": ("n8", "He says very little. He has said that sometimes he just shuts down, and won't talk to anyone. He lets the stage do the talking. A theatre student, still performing."),
    "t5": ("n8", "Underneath the money, the chains and the helicopters, the story his fans hold on to is a simple one. A stubborn boy who believed in himself before anybody else did.", dict(span=(147, 176))),
    "e1": ("n8", "Remember the boy on the parlour floor? 2022. No plan. No idea what's coming next."),
    "e2": ("n8", "That boy was me. I made this documentary."),
    "e3": ("o7", "Ololade. Wealth has arrived. And sometimes, before it arrives in your pocket, it has arrived in your head. Stay stubborn."),
    "e4": ("o7", "If this story moved you, subscribe to Naija Untold. And tell me in the comments: which Asake song found you when you needed it?"),
}
